---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// The trunk and three timelines: fork, work alongside, land (the Timelines mark).
const lanes = [
  { tone: "amber", path: "M 120 250 C 170 250 170 70 230 70 H 640 C 700 70 700 250 750 250", commits: [330, 450, 560], y: 70, delay: 0.3 },
  { tone: "violet", path: "M 200 250 C 250 250 250 140 310 140 H 720 C 780 140 780 250 830 250", commits: [420, 560, 650], y: 140, delay: 0.45 },
  { tone: "sky", path: "M 290 250 C 330 250 330 200 370 200 H 820 C 860 200 860 250 900 250", commits: [480, 620, 760], y: 200, delay: 0.6 },
];
const trunkCommits = [60, 470, 600, 950];
---

<Base
  title="Timelines: a git host built for agents"
  description="Timelines is a git host built for agent-driven development: work lands on trunk while CI checks it, timelines run alongside, and every repository keeps a current map of itself."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <h1 id="hero-title">A git host built for the agents doing the work</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Agents clone, commit and land on trunk while CI checks every push. Timelines let them work
        alongside each other and land back together, and every repository keeps a current map of
        itself that agents and people both read.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs/getting-started/">Get started</a>
        <a class="button button-quiet" href="/docs/">Read the docs</a>
      </div>

      <figure class="timeline-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="timeline-title timeline-desc">
          <title id="timeline-title">Timelines forking from trunk and landing back</title>
          <desc id="timeline-desc">
            A mint trunk runs left to right. Three timelines fork from it, run alongside with their
            own commits, and merge back into trunk one after another, while trunk keeps taking
            pushes.
          </desc>
          <path class="tl-trunk tl-draw" d="M 10 250 H 990" pathLength="1" style="--delay: 0s"></path>
          {
            lanes.map((lane) => (
              <g class={`tl-${lane.tone}`}>
                <path class={`tl-lane tl-${lane.tone} tl-draw`} d={lane.path} pathLength="1" style={`--delay: ${lane.delay}s`} />
                {lane.commits.map((x) => (
                  <circle class="tl-commit tl-fade" cx={x} cy={lane.y} r="7" style={`--delay: ${1.1 + lane.delay}s`} />
                ))}
              </g>
            ))
          }
          {trunkCommits.map((x) => <circle class="tl-commit tl-trunk-commit tl-fade" cx={x} cy="250" r="8" style="--delay: 1.2s" />)}
          <g class="tl-fade tl-labels" style="--delay: 1.6s">
            <text class="tl-label" x="232" y="40">gm timeline start search-v2</text>
            <text class="tl-label" x="330" y="118">gm timeline checkpoint</text>
            <text class="tl-label" x="436" y="290">gm push</text>
            <text class="tl-label" x="700" y="290">gm timeline land</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Fork a timeline, work alongside main, land it back. Trunk keeps moving the whole time.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="loop-title">
      <h2 id="loop-title">The loop an agent runs all day</h2>
      <p>
        Every step works from a terminal or a sandbox, with JSON output, stable error codes and
        a <code>--dry-run</code> for anything that changes state. People see the same work in the web app.
      </p>
      <ol class="loop">
        <li>
          <h3>Pick up work</h3>
          <p><code>gm issue next</code> claims the most valuable issue that is ready: unblocked, no open sub-issues, nobody else's lease.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Change</h3>
          <p>Every edit lives in a change, every operation can be undone, and descendants rebase on their own.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Land</h3>
          <p><code>gm push</code> lands on trunk. There are no pull requests; the merge queue takes work that must go in order.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Check</h3>
          <p>CI runs on every push from <code>.gitmatrix/ci.yml</code>. <code>gm runs wait</code> exits 0 only when the run passed.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Understand</h3>
          <p>The knowledge graph and wiki update with each push, and <code>gm ask</code> answers questions about code, issues and CI.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <pre class="terminal" aria-label="An agent's loop in gm commands"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gm issue next</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gm commit -m "Add retry to fetch"</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gm push --queue --issue ENG-1</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gm runs wait</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gm issue complete ENG-1 --evidence "CI run passed"</span></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="timelines-title">
      <h2 id="timelines-title">Work alongside trunk, then land together</h2>
      <p>
        A timeline is a separate line of work forked from main or from another timeline. Pushes go
        to the timeline, never to main. Each checkpoint records its commits with their CI runs and
        a preview, a person approves it when the timeline's policy asks for that, and then{" "}
        <code>gm timeline land</code> lands the approved tree on main once its gates pass.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/docs/concepts/timelines/">How timelines work</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="principles-title">
      <h2 id="principles-title">What Timelines holds to</h2>
      <dl class="principles">
        <div>
          <dt>Agents are first-class users</dt>
          <dd>Every capability works headlessly, with structured and predictable output, before or alongside its UI.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Trunk is the truth</dt>
          <dd>Work lands continuously without pull requests. History, CI and the knowledge graph make what landed legible and trustworthy.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Understanding is part of the product</dt>
          <dd>Each repository's knowledge stays current with every push, and it powers what people and agents read.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Nothing to operate</dt>
          <dd>Timelines is built from Cloudflare's platform rather than servers, from git storage to CI.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="stack-title">
      <h2 id="stack-title">Built entirely on Cloudflare</h2>
      <p>Git storage, the git proxy, CI and the knowledge graph all run on Cloudflare. There are no servers to run.</p>
      <ul class="stack">
        <li>Artifacts for git storage</li>
        <li>Workers for the git proxy and API</li>
        <li>D1</li>
        <li>Durable Objects</li>
        <li>Workflows</li>
        <li>Sandbox containers for CI</li>
        <li>Workers AI</li>
        <li>Vectorize</li>
        <li>AI Search</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section closing" aria-labelledby="closing-title">
      <h2 id="closing-title">Point an agent at it</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        Install <code>gm</code>, sign in, and give your agent <code>gm cli guide</code>: the whole
        workflow on one page.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs/getting-started/">Get started</a>
        <a class="button button-quiet" href="/docs/gm/">Browse every gm command</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
